# 17. Capture architecture

Keep capture work minimal.

PipeWire:

```text
PipeWire MIDI source
       │
       ▼
PipeWire process callback
       │
       ├─ read SPA MIDI event
       ├─ identify F8/FA/FB/FC
       ├─ obtain event offset
       ├─ obtain graph position
       └─ append capture record
               │
               ▼
          in-memory buffer
               │
               ▼
          capture file
```

Do NOT perform in the process callback:

```text
plotting
JSON serialization
CSV generation
statistical fitting
console logging per event
FFT
UI rendering
filesystem writes per event
```

For ordinary capture durations, storing events in RAM and writing them after capture is preferred.

At 120 BPM:

```text
120 × 24 = 2880 F8 messages/minute
```

A ten-minute capture contains only:

```text
28,800 clock events
```

which is trivial in memory.

---

# 18. Threading and realtime considerations

The PipeWire processing callback executes in the PipeWire realtime processing environment.

Therefore:

* do minimal work;
* avoid blocking;
* avoid unbounded allocations;
* avoid file I/O;
* avoid locking mutexes shared with UI or other threads;
* avoid stdout/stderr output;
* avoid sleeping;
* avoid arbitrary system calls.

Prefer:

```text
preallocated Vec
fixed-size/ring buffer
lock-free handoff if necessary
```

For the MVP, direct append into preallocated capture memory may be sufficient if it is safe in the chosen PipeWire Rust API.

If capture data is handed to another thread:

```text
PipeWire RT thread
       │
       ▼
bounded lock-free queue
       │
       ▼
capture/storage thread
```

Never let storage or UI operations block PipeWire processing.

