# 50. Core design principle

Preserve strict separation:

```text
MIDI SOURCE
     ↓
CAPTURE BACKEND
     ↓
RAW TIMING INFORMATION
     ↓
NORMALIZED EVENT TIMES
     ↓
CLOCK MODEL
     ↓
STATISTICAL ANALYSIS
     ↓
PRESENTATION
```

Never alter raw event timing to make results look cleaner.

Never timestamp PipeWire MIDI based merely on when the userspace callback executes.

Never quantize MIDI events to PipeWire cycle boundaries.

Never silently discard anomalies.

Never assume nominal BPM.

Never use wall-clock time for jitter analysis.

Never equate MIDI Clock jitter directly with audible note-on jitter.

The primary PipeWire result should represent:

> **the timing stability of MIDI Clock events as presented at the PipeWire application boundary, using the graph position and per-event timing information supplied by PipeWire.**

The optional ALSA backend should provide a lower-level reference point for determining whether additional timing variability appears elsewhere in the Linux MIDI stack.

